.\" Manpage for ia_upload.
.TH IA_UPLOAD 1 "2026-09-03" "ia_upload 1.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
ia_upload \- resilient bulk uploader for archive.org items
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ia_upload.py
.I IDENTIFIER SOURCE
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ia_upload
uploads a file or a directory tree to a single archive.org item over IA's S3
interface, and is built to survive the conditions that defeat
.BR "ia upload" (1)
on large collections: multi-gigabyte files, slow links, and IA nodes that
stall or return 503 under load.
.PP
It is safe to interrupt and re-run at any point. Every run begins by reading
the item's current file list and comparing MD5 sums, so a file that already
landed intact is never sent twice. MD5 sums are cached between runs, keyed by
path, size and mtime, so a re-run does not re-read the whole tree.
.
.SS "Why this exists"
The
.B internetarchive
Python library hardcodes a 120 second request timeout
.RB ( item.py ,
in
.BR upload_file ).
That value is a
.I read
timeout: it bounds how long the client waits for IA's reply
.I after
the request body has been sent. IA writes the object to a storage node and
checksums it before replying, so any sufficiently large file will exceed 120
seconds while the transfer is in fact working perfectly. The result is
.PP
.RS
.nf
requests.exceptions.ReadTimeout: (ReadTimeoutError("HTTPSConnectionPool(
host='s3.us.archive.org', port=443): Read timed out. (read timeout=120)"))
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B ia_upload
scales that timeout with file size (see
.BR "TIMEOUTS" )
and, when a timeout does occur, re-reads the item's file list to check whether
the upload actually succeeded before spending another full transfer on it.
.PP
Note that the body itself is always streamed from disk in small blocks by
.BR requests ,
never buffered in memory. Chunking the read side is neither necessary nor
sufficient to fix a read timeout.
.
.SS "Relationship to ia upload"
The
.BR ia (1)
command-line client exposes no timeout option at all, so the 120 second limit
cannot be raised from it. Its
.B \-\-retries
option retries only on HTTP 503 and on IA's "S3 overloaded" pre-check; a
.B ReadTimeout
propagates immediately and is never retried, however high the count is set.
.B ia_upload
owns its retry loop and treats timeouts, connection resets, and all retryable
HTTP statuses as recoverable.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.
.SS "What to send"
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
List what would be uploaded and exit. Contacts the item to read its file list
but sends nothing, and needs no credentials. Always worth running first.
.TP
.BR \-\-metadata ", " \-m " " \fIKEY:VALUE\fR
Item metadata, repeatable. Metadata is only applied by the request that
creates the item, so this has no effect on an item that already exists; use
.B ia metadata
for those. Repeating a key builds a list.
.IP
.B "An item with no title or description is frequently rejected as spam."
Set at least
.B \-m 'title:...'
and
.B \-m 'description:...'
when creating one.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " GLOB"
Skip files and directories whose name matches this shell glob, repeatable.
Matching is against the base name, not the full path. Excluded directories are
not descended into.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-hidden
Include dotfiles and dot-directories, which are skipped by default.
.
.SS Derivation
.TP
.B \-\-derive
Queue IA's derivation task once every file has been sent. Derivation is
skipped automatically if any file failed or the run was interrupted, so that
IA does not derive a half-populated item. Without this flag, no derive is
queued at all and you can run
.B ia derive
.I IDENTIFIER
yourself later.
.TP
.B \-\-reduced\-priority
Submit the derive at low priority. Slower to process, less likely to run into
IA's task rate limits on a large item.
.
.SS "Retries and pacing"
.TP
.BI \-\-retries " N"
Attempts per file (default: 12). Backoff is exponential with jitter, so 12
attempts already spans several hours; there is little reason to raise it.
.TP
.BI \-\-retry\-wait " SEC"
Delay before the first retry (default: 15). Doubles after each failure.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-retry\-wait " SEC"
Ceiling on the backoff delay (default: 600).
.TP
.BI \-\-wait\-between " SEC"
Pause between files (default: 0). Raise this if IA starts returning 503
consistently; it is gentler than letting every file fail into its retry loop.
.
.SS TIMEOUTS
The read timeout for a file is computed as
.PP
.RS
.BI min( \-\-max\-timeout ,
.IB \-\-read\-timeout " + " \-\-timeout\-per\-gb " * size_in_GB" )
.RE
.PP
With the defaults, a 100 MB file gets 630 seconds and a 20 GB file gets 6600.
.TP
.BI \-\-connect\-timeout " SEC"
TCP connect timeout (default: 30).
.TP
.BI \-\-read\-timeout " SEC"
Base response timeout (default: 600).
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout\-per\-gb " SEC"
Additional response timeout per gigabyte (default: 300).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-timeout " SEC"
Ceiling on the response timeout (default: 7200). Must not be below
.BR \-\-read\-timeout .
.
.SS Bookkeeping
.TP
.BI \-\-state " PATH"
Resume-state file (default:
.BI .ia_upload_ IDENTIFIER .json
in the working directory). Holds cached MD5 sums and the list of files IA has
accepted. Written atomically, mode 0600, and safe to delete at any time;
doing so only costs a re-hash of the tree.
.TP
.BI \-\-log " PATH"
Append log output to this file as well as to standard output. Anything
resembling an IA S3 credential is redacted before it is written.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify
Skip the final pass that re-reads the item and confirms every uploaded file's
MD5.
.TP
.B \-\-trust\-state
Skip files the state file records as accepted, even when IA's file list does
not show them yet. IA's file list can lag minutes behind an accepted upload,
and without this flag those files are sent again. Only files for which IA
returned success are ever recorded, so this is sound, but the file list is
the only real evidence the bytes are still there, which is why it is opt-in.
Re-run without it once the item has settled.
.TP
.BR \-\-yes ", " \-y
Do not prompt before overwriting a remote file whose contents differ. Required
for unattended runs that would overwrite; without it, a non-interactive run
that would overwrite exits 2 rather than guessing.
.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Everything uploaded and verified, or there was nothing to do.
.TP
.B 1
Some files failed, or could not be confirmed on the item afterwards. Re-run
the identical command to retry only those.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage, bad identifier, unreadable source, missing credentials, a filename
IA will reject, or a refused overwrite.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted. Re-run to continue where it stopped.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B IA_ACCESS_KEY_ID
.TQ
.B IA_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
IA S3 credentials. Both must be set, or neither. Usually unnecessary: running
.B ia configure
once writes them to the config file instead.
.TP
.B IA_CONFIG_FILE
Alternative path to the
.B ia
configuration file.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/internetarchive/ia.ini
Credentials written by
.BR "ia configure" .
.TP
.IB .ia_upload_ IDENTIFIER .json
Default resume state, in the working directory.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Check first. This sends nothing and needs no credentials:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ia_upload.py Game-maps-collection ./maps/ --dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create the item and upload a tree, logging to a file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ia_upload.py Game-maps-collection ./maps/ \\
    -m 'title:Game Maps Collection' \\
    -m 'description:Community map packs, 1999-2007.' \\
    -m 'mediatype:software' \\
    -m 'collection:opensource' \\
    --derive --log upload.log
.fi
.RE
.PP
Resume after an interruption. This is the identical command, and it re-sends
only what is missing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ia_upload.py Game-maps-collection ./maps/ --derive --log upload.log
.fi
.RE
.PP
A link that keeps dropping, or an item IA is throttling:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ia_upload.py Game-maps-collection ./maps/ \\
    --retries 30 --wait-between 10 --max-retry-wait 900
.fi
.RE
.PP
Skip editor litter and OS metadata:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ia_upload.py Game-maps-collection ./maps/ \\
    --exclude '*.tmp' --exclude '.DS_Store' --exclude 'Thumbs.db'
.fi
.RE
.
.SH NOTES
.SS "Migrating from ia upload"
.TP
.B \-\-checksum
Default behaviour, and cheaper: the item's file list is read once per run
rather than per file, and local MD5 sums are cached between runs.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Always on. Every request carries a
.B Content\-MD5
header computed from the cached sum, so IA verifies the object server-side.
This is strictly better than the library's own
.BR verify=True ,
which re-reads the entire file from disk on every attempt.
.TP
.BI \-\-retries " N"
Use
.BI \-\-retries " N"
here, but a large value is not needed: this retry loop covers timeouts and
connection failures, which the library's does not, and backs off
exponentially rather than sleeping a fixed interval.
.TP
.BI \-\-sleep " SEC"
Closest equivalent is
.BI \-\-retry\-wait " SEC"
(the first delay, which then doubles up to
.BR \-\-max\-retry\-wait ).
.PP
So
.B "ia upload ID DIR --checksum --verify --retries 150 --sleep 15"
becomes simply
.B "ia_upload.py ID DIR" .
The defaults already cover it.
.
.SS "Things that are skipped"
Symbolic links are never followed, so nothing outside
.I SOURCE
can be uploaded by accident. Empty files are skipped: IA accepts them, but
they are almost always an artifact of an interrupted copy. Both are reported.
.
.SS "Filenames"
Remote names are the paths relative to
.IR SOURCE ,
with
.B /
separators, so the directory structure is preserved on the item. Before
anything is sent, names are checked for control characters, over-long names,
empty or space-padded path segments, and case-insensitive collisions. IA
rejects or silently mangles all of these, and they are far cheaper to find
now than four hours into a run.
.
.SS Overwriting
Uploading a file whose name already exists on the item but whose contents
differ replaces it, and IA retains the previous copy as a versioned file that
still counts against the item's size. The run stops to confirm before doing
this unless
.B \-\-yes
is given.
.
.SS "Verification lag"
IA's file list is updated asynchronously, so the verification pass at the end
of a run can legitimately report files it cannot yet see. This is usually
timing rather than loss; re-running later confirms them, and anything
genuinely missing is re-sent. See
.BR \-\-trust\-state .
.
